  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0173\" consists of 9 tables.\nThe \"blood\" table is used to store information about blood types and their compatibility with other types. It has two columns: \"blood_type\" which is a primary key and cannot be null, and \"compatible_types\" which stores a string of blood types that the given blood type is compatible with.\nThe \"hospital\" table stores information about hospitals. It has several columns including \"hospital_id\" as the primary key, \"hospital_name\", \"address\", \"contact_number\", \"email\", \"head_doctor_name\", \"services_offered\", and \"emergency_services_availability\" which indicates whether the hospital offers emergency services.\nThe \"private_facility\" table stores information about private medical facilities. It has similar columns to the \"hospital\" table, but without the \"emergency_services_availability\" column.\nThe \"donor\" table stores information about blood donors. It has columns for \"donor_id\" as the primary key, \"first_name\", \"last_name\", \"date_of_birth\", \"gender\", \"contact_number\", \"email\", and \"home_address\".\nThe \"donor_medical\" table stores medical information about donors. It has columns for \"donor_medical_id\" as the primary key, \"donor_id\" which references the \"donor\" table, \"allergies\", \"weight\", \"blood_type\", \"medical_condition\", and \"medical_history\" as well as \"insurance_details\".\nThe \"patient\" table stores information about patients. It has similar columns to the \"donor\" table.\nThe \"patient_medical\" table stores medical information about patients. It has columns for \"patient_medical_id\" as the primary key, \"patient_id\" which references the \"patient\" table, \"blood_type\", \"allergies\", \"medical_condition\", \"medical_history\", and \"insurance_details\".\nThe \"blood_transfusion\" table stores information about blood transfusions. It has columns for \"transfusion_id\" as the primary key, \"patient_medical_id\" which references the \"patient_medical\" table, \"hospital_id\" which references the \"hospital\" table, \"private_facility_id\" which references the \"private_facility\" table, \"admission_date\", \"discharge_date\", and \"transfusion_date\" as well as \"room_number\".\nThe \"blood_donations\" table stores information about blood donations. It has columns for \"blood_donation_id\" as the primary key, \"donor_medical_id\" which references the \"donor_medical\" table, \"hospital_id\" which references the \"hospital\" table, \"private_facility_id\" which references the \"private_facility\" table, \"collection_date\", \"expiry_date\", \"contaminated\", \"blood_description\", \"component_type\", \"anticoagulant_used\", and \"quantity\".\nEach table has primary keys and foreign keys to enforce entity integrity and referential integrity. For instance, the \"donor_medical\" table has a foreign key \"donor_id\" which references the \"donor\" table, and the \"blood_type\" which references the \"blood\" table. This ensures that each donor medical record is associated with a valid donor and a valid blood type. Similarly, the \"patient_medical\" table has foreign keys \"patient_id\" and \"blood_type\" which reference the \"patient\" and \"blood\" tables respectively. The \"blood_transfusion\" and \"blood_donations\" tables have foreign keys referencing the \"patient_medical\", \"hospital\", and \"private_facility\" tables respectively.",
  "gold_question": "Blood Bank Management System\n\n1) Background / Context\n\nThe Blood Bank Management System is focused on managing healthcare data related to blood donations and transfusions. It serves as a central repository for tracking information about donors, patients, hospitals, and private facilities. The system leverages a SQL-based database to efficiently manage complex datasets involved in blood bank operations.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n- Facilitate efficient data management and querying for blood-related healthcare data.\n- Support the execution of complex queries concerning donors, patients, and medical institutions.\n- Enhance the management and tracking of blood donations and transfusions.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Healthcare Professionals: Doctors and nurses using the system to manage blood transfusion and donation records.\n- Administrative Staff: Personnel maintaining hospital and private facility records.\n- IT Staff: Database administrators managing the SQL-based system.\n- Donors and Patients: Indirect users whose data is managed by the system.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Donor Management: Record and manage donor information including personal details and medical history.\n- Patient Management: Record and manage patient profiles, including medical conditions and blood compatibility.\n- Hospital and Facility Management: Maintain records of hospitals and private facilities, including services offered and emergency capabilities.\n- Blood Inventory Management: Track details about blood donations, including collection dates, expiry, contamination status, and component types.\n- Blood Transfusion Tracking: Monitor transfusion records, including admission and discharge details.\n- Query Execution: Support complex queries across the dataset for reporting and analysis.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: Support substantial volumes of data and operational queries without performance degradation.\n- Security: Ensure secure handling and storage of sensitive healthcare information.\n- Data Integrity: Maintain accurate, consistent data across all transactions and operations.\n- Reliability: Provide consistent service availability to support healthcare operations.\n- Usability: Simple and intuitive querying and data management interfaces for various users.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0173.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0173\nCREATE TABLE `blood` (\n  `blood_type` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `compatible_types` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`blood_type`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `hospital` (\n  `hospital_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `hospital_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_number` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `head_doctor_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `services_offered` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `emergency_services_availability` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`hospital_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `private_facility` (\n  `private_facility_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `hospital_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_number` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `head_doctor_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `services_offered` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`private_facility_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `donor` (\n  `donor_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_of_birth` date NOT NULL,\n  `gender` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_number` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `home_address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`donor_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `donor_medical` (\n  `donor_medical_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `donor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `allergies` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `weight` decimal(5,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `blood_type` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `medical_condition` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `medical_history` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `insurance_details` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`donor_medical_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `donor_medical_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`donor_id`) REFERENCES `donor` (`donor_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `donor_medical_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`blood_type`) REFERENCES `blood` (`blood_type`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `patient` (\n  `patient_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_of_birth` date NOT NULL,\n  `gender` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_number` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `home_address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`patient_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `patient_medical` (\n  `patient_medical_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `patient_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `blood_type` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `allergies` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `medical_condition` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `medical_history` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `insurance_details` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`patient_medical_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `patient_medical_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`patient_id`) REFERENCES `patient` (`patient_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `patient_medical_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`blood_type`) REFERENCES `blood` (`blood_type`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `blood_transfusion` (\n  `transfusion_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `patient_medical_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `hospital_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `private_facility_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `admission_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `discharge_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `transfusion_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `room_number` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`transfusion_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `blood_transfusion_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`patient_medical_id`) REFERENCES `patient_medical` (`patient_medical_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `blood_transfusion_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`hospital_id`) REFERENCES `hospital` (`hospital_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `blood_transfusion_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`private_facility_id`) REFERENCES `private_facility` (`private_facility_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `blood_donations` (\n  `blood_donation_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `donor_medical_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `hospital_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `private_facility_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `collection_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `expiry_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `contaminated` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `blood_description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `component_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `anticoagulant_used` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `quantity` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`blood_donation_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `blood_donations_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`donor_medical_id`) REFERENCES `donor_medical` (`donor_medical_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `blood_donations_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`hospital_id`) REFERENCES `hospital` (`hospital_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `blood_donations_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`private_facility_id`) REFERENCES `private_facility` (`private_facility_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
